When Was PPF Invented? The History of Paint Protection Film
Paint protection film (PPF) was invented in the 1960s. The U.S. military originally engineered the technology to shield helicopter rotor blades from harsh sand and flying debris during the Vietnam War. Automakers and detailing professionals began applying film to road vehicles in the early 1990s, triggering decades of continuous material innovation.

Why PPF's Origin Story Matters
Most drivers assume paint protection film is a modern luxury car trend. In reality, its roots lie in defense engineering rather than civilian auto care. Understanding this history reveals why today's high-grade films offer such incredible impact resistance and highlights why product quality can differ so drastically across the market.

